        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. What You Sacrifice (and What You Don't Have To) Under $1,000</h2>
        <p>
          Not every trade-off at this price point matters equally. Some are
          genuine long-term risks worth paying more to avoid, and others are
          minor conveniences you can happily live without. Here's how we
          break down the real differences between budget and premium
          machines, based on what the specifications actually imply rather
          than what the marketing claims.
        </p>

        <h3>Motor Durability & Continuous Duty HP</h3>
        <p>
          This is the trade-off that matters most. Continuous horsepower
          (CHP) is the rating that tells you how much sustained load a motor
          can handle without overheating, as opposed to peak HP, which is
          often an inflated marketing number measured in short bursts.
          Budget machines in the <strong>1.6 to 2.0 CHP</strong> range are
          built for walking and shouldn't be pushed into regular running use;
          motors in this range are light-duty and tend to run hot and loud
          when consistently pushed past their comfort zone. Machines in the
          <strong>2.5 to 3.0 CHP</strong> range have enough headroom for both
          walking and jogging without excess strain, which is why we weight
          this spec heavily in our rankings, and why we flag it when a maker,
          as Horizon does for the T202 on our list, publishes no motor rating
          at all.
        </p>

        <h3>Deck Cushioning Quality</h3>
        <p>
          Premium machines often use multi-zone or elastomer-based cushioning
          systems tuned to be softer at the heel-strike zone and firmer
          toward the front for push-off. Budget machines typically use a
          simpler, uniform cushioning layer, or in the cheapest cases, very
          little cushioning at all. This isn't necessarily a dealbreaker for
          walking-focused use, but it becomes noticeable during longer
          running sessions, where impact accumulates. Walking-only machines
          like the Exerpeutic Senior Fitness Treadmill on our list matter
          less here, because they are not designed for high-impact use in
          the first place.
        </p>

        <h3>Touchscreen vs. Basic LED Console</h3>
        <p>
          This is one of the sacrifices that matters least for durability,
          but most for daily enjoyment. A basic LED console, like you'll find
          on the Horizon T202 or Schwinn 810, is simple, reliable, and rarely
          the point of failure on a treadmill. Smaller touchscreens on
          connected budget machines, like the ProForm Carbon T7 or
          NordicTrack T Series 6.5 S, add real engagement value through
          trainer-led content, but they also add another component that can
          eventually need replacing, and much of their value depends on
          maintaining a paid subscription.
        </p>

        <h3>Warranty Length</h3>
        <p>
          This is where the budget-versus-premium gap is often starkest.
          Premium machines frequently offer lifetime frame and motor
          warranties with multi-year parts and labor coverage. Budget
          machines under $1,000 typically offer <strong>1-2 years on parts</strong>
          and often just <strong>90 days on labor</strong>, with the motor
          itself sometimes covered longer than the rest of the machine. Read
          the fine print before buying, and factor a shorter warranty window
          into your overall value calculation.
        </p>

        <h3>Build Materials & Long-Term Rattle</h3>
        <p>
          Budget machines generally use more plastic in side rails, consoles,
          and folding hinges, and lighter-gauge steel in the frame. This
          doesn't automatically mean a machine will fall apart, but it does
          mean the margin for manufacturing inconsistency is smaller.
          Machines with hydraulic-assist folding mechanisms, like the Horizon
          T202, tend to develop less play over time than
          machines with simple spring-assist or manual folding.
        </p>

