      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mt-12">
        <!-- SECTION 4: Stretching Your Budget -->
        <h2 id="stretching-your-budget" class="scroll-mt-24">
          How to Stretch Your Budget Further
        </h2>
        <p>
          Getting the most treadmill for your money isn't just about which
          model you choose, it's also about when and how you buy it. A
          little strategy can move you from a $399 walking-only machine into
          the territory of a $699 machine with a real running-capable motor,
          without spending a dollar more.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Time your purchase around major sales windows.</strong>
          Black Friday and Cyber Monday consistently produce the deepest treadmill
          discounts of the year, often <strong>20-30% off list price</strong> on
          exactly the models featured in this guide. Prime Day in July is
          the second-biggest window, and early January, when everyone is
          buying fitness equipment for New Year's resolutions but retailers
          are also trying to clear prior-generation inventory, is a
          surprisingly good time to find open-box and clearance deals.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Consider refurbished and open-box units carefully.</strong>
          Manufacturer-refurbished treadmills, sold directly through the brand
          or through major retailers, often come with a real (if shortened) warranty
          and can save 20-40% off new pricing. Be more cautious with
          third-party open-box listings that don't specify who inspected and
          repackaged the unit; ask specifically what testing was done before
          resale.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Prioritize motor quality over touchscreen gimmicks.</strong>
          It's tempting to be drawn in by a bigger screen or a flashier console,
          but a treadmill's screen is not what determines whether it's still
          working in three years. If you're choosing between two machines at
          a similar price and one has a stronger, better-reviewed motor while
          the other has a nicer display, choose the motor every time.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Think carefully about extended warranties.</strong> Many retailers
          push extended warranty add-ons at checkout for $50-100. Whether
          these are worth it depends heavily on the base warranty already
          included. If a machine only ships with 90 days of labor coverage,
          an extended plan that adds two to three years of labor protection
          can be a reasonable hedge, especially on connected machines with
          more complex electronics. If the base warranty already covers the
          motor for 5+ years, an extended plan adds less real value.
        </p>

        <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8 not-prose">
          <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-amber-700 mb-2">
            Budget Buying Tip
          </p>
          <p class="text-amber-900 leading-relaxed m-0">
            Be wary of any treadmill under $1,000 with a weight capacity
            listed well below its category average (under <strong
              >200 lbs</strong
            > for a full-size folding machine, for example). An unusually low
            weight rating relative to price is often a signal that the manufacturer
            cut costs on the frame and motor to hit an aggressive price point,
            not just a marketing quirk. Match the rated capacity to your heaviest
            household user with real margin to spare, not just enough to squeak
            by.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: Longevity Tips -->
        <h2 id="longevity-tips" class="scroll-mt-24">
          Making a Budget Treadmill Last Longer
        </h2>
        <p>
          Budget treadmills have less margin for neglect than premium
          machines built with heavier-duty components. A few consistent
          maintenance habits are the difference between a machine that lasts
          five-plus years and one that starts developing problems within
          months.
        </p>

        <h3>Lubrication Schedule</h3>
        <p>
          This matters more, not less, on a budget machine. Most
          manufacturers recommend lubricating the belt and deck every
          <strong>150-200 miles</strong> of use, or roughly every three
          months for a household walking regularly. Skipping this is one of
          the fastest ways to accelerate belt and motor wear, since a dry
          deck creates more friction, which forces the motor to work harder
          and generates excess heat. Silicone-based lubricant made
          specifically for treadmills, applied under the belt edges per the
          manufacturer's instructions, takes about ten minutes and is by far
          the highest-leverage maintenance task you can do.
        </p>

        <h3>Respect the Duty Cycle Rating</h3>
        <p>
          Every motor has a duty cycle, meaning the amount of continuous use
          it's designed to handle before needing a cooldown period. Budget
          motors generally have lower duty cycle tolerance than premium
          motors. Running a 1.6 CHP walking-focused machine for back-to-back
          two-hour sessions, or letting multiple household members use it in
          rapid succession all day, pushes it well outside its intended use
          and accelerates wear far faster than the specs would suggest.
        </p>

        <h3>Use a Dedicated Outlet and Surge Protector</h3>
        <p>
          Treadmill motors draw significant current, especially at startup
          and during incline changes. Plugging into a circuit shared with
          other high-draw appliances can cause voltage fluctuations that
          stress the motor's control board over time. A dedicated,
          properly-grounded outlet paired with a surge protector rated for
          the treadmill's amperage is inexpensive insurance against
          electrical damage, particularly during storms or power grid
          fluctuations.
        </p>

        <h3>Check Belt Tension Regularly</h3>
        <p>
          A belt that's too loose will slip under load, especially during
          incline use, while a belt that's too tight puts unnecessary strain
          on the motor and rollers. Most manufacturers include tensioning
          bolts at the rear of the deck; check monthly by lifting the belt
          edge (it should lift 2-3 inches with moderate effort) and adjusting
          in quarter-turn increments if it feels loose or overly stiff.
        </p>

